WEBVTT

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:29.510
"I shall never forget the weekend Laura died. The silver sun burned through the sky like a huge magnifying glass. It was the hottest Sunday in my recollection. I felt as if I were the only human being left in New York for Laura's horrible death.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:56.050
I, Waldo Lidecker, was the only one who really knew her, and I had just begun to write Laura's story when another of those detectives came to see me. I had him wait so I could watch him through the half-open door.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.660
I noted that his attention was fixed upon my clock.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:16.740
There was only one other in existence.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.390
And that was in Laura's apartment.

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:25.290
The very room where she was murdered.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.090
Careful there.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:29.280
That stuff is priceless.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.670
Come in here, please.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:41.340
Lidecker?

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:42.940
Ah, you recognize me?

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.020
I'm splendid.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:48.560
Sit down, please.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.900
Nice little place you have here, Mr. Lidecker.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.050
It's lavish.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.210
But I call it home.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:57.690
I suppose you'll hear about the Laura Hunt murder.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:00.000
Yesterday morning, after Laura's body was found, I was

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:06.450
questioned by Sergeants McCafferty and Schultz. And I stated, "On Friday night, Laura had

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:12.410
a dinner engagement with me, after which she was ostensibly going out of town. She phoned

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:16.720
and cancelled our engagement at exactly seven o'clock. After that I..."

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:23.630
"You ate a lonely dinner, then got into the tub to read. Why did you write it down? Afraid

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:30.000
you'd forget it?" "I am the most widely misquoted man in America, and my friend

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.760
do it, I resent it. From Sergeants McAvery and Schultz, I shall find it intolerable. Hand

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:47.080
me that washcloth, please, Mr.. Mr.. McPherson. McPherson. McPherson. Mark McPherson. The

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:50.920
Siege of Babylon, Long Island. The gangster with the machine gun killed three policemen.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:54.020
I told the story over the air and wrote a column about it. Are you the one with the

00:03:54.020 --> 00:04:00.000
leg full of lead? The man who walked right in and got him? Yeah. Well, well. Hand me my

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:10.800
please. You have a pretty good memory, Mr. Leidecker. I always liked that detective with the silver

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.570
shinbone. Thanks. I hope you won't have any reason to change your mind about me. Have you any more

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:22.930
questions? Yeah, just one. Two years ago, in your October 17th column, you started out to write a book

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:27.160
review. But at the bottom of the column, you switched over to the Harrington murder case.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.000
Are the processes of the creative mind now a

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.020
the jurisdiction of the police. You said Harrington was rubbed out with a shotgun

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:39.940
loaded with buckshot. The way Laura Hunt was murdered, night before last. Did I?

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:46.900
Yeah, but he was really killed with a sash weight. No ordinary. My version was

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:53.380
obviously superior. I never bother with details, you know. I do. Well, so long.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:05:00.000
Mind if I go with you? What for? Murder is my favorite crime.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:08.270
I write about it regularly, and I know you'll have to visit everyone on your list of suspects. I'd like to study their reactions.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:13.770
You're on the list yourself, you know. Good. To have overlooked me would have been a pointed insult.

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:20.570
You're not the sort of man one would insult, Mr. Lydekker. Do you really suspect me? Yes.

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:30.000
McPherson, if you know anything about faces, look at mine. How singularly innocent

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:40.960
I looked this morning. Have you ever seen such candid eyes, something you confiscated in a raid

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:50.170
on the kindergarten? Takes a lot of control. Would you like to try it? No, thanks. Were you in love

00:05:50.170 --> 00:06:00.000
with Laura Hunt, Mr. Lidegger? Is she in love with you? Laura considered me the wisest, the wittiest,

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:05.120
most interesting man she'd ever met. I was in complete accord with her on that point.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:13.280
She thought me also the kindest, the gentlest, most sympathetic man in the world.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:20.940
Did you agree with her there too? McPherson, you won't understand this, but I tried to become

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:30.000
the kindest, gentlest, the most sympathetic man in the world. Have any luck? Let me put it this

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:37.970
way. I should be sincerely sorry to see my neighbor's children devoured by wolves. Shall we go?

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:04.310
- Good morning, Lieutenant.

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:05.630
- Good morning, Mrs. Treadwell.

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:06.350
- Good morning, Anne.

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:08.570
- I've just taken over the Laura Hunt case.

00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:10.350
- Won't you sit down?

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:11.690
- Thanks.

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:12.930
I have all the reports,

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.310
but there are a few more questions I'd like to ask.

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:17.010
- Certainly, I'll do anything I can to help.

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:19.320
- You were fond of your niece, Mrs. Treadwell?

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:20.770
- Why, I adored her.

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:21.610
Cigarette?

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:22.450
- Thanks.

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:24.410
You collapsed when you identified the body.

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:25.630
You can quite understand that.

00:07:25.630 --> 00:07:28.890
Shotgun loaded with buckshot, close range.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:30.000
Not very nice to look at.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:31.340
It's horrible.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:34.540
Her maid Bessie, I suppose she was devoted to Miss Hunt.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:35.540
Oh, she worshipped her.

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:37.460
Laura had had her for years.

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:40.650
I'll never forget her scream when she saw Laura lying there.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:45.040
Did you approve of Miss Hunt's coming marriage to Mr. Carpenter?

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:46.360
Why?

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:47.720
Shouldn't I approve?

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:49.150
I don't know.

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:52.660
What is your relationship with Mr. Carpenter?

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:54.940
What do you mean?

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:58.020
What I mean is he's been a frequent guest in your home.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:07:59.020
Is he an acquaintance?

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:00.000
A friend?

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:04.990
love with him? This is beginning to assume fabulous aspects. Oh, shut up, Waldo. What

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:12.860
are you driving at? The truth, Mrs. Dreadwell. Are you in love with him? I know. I'm, I'm

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:17.820
very fond of Mr. Carpenter, of course. Everybody is. I'm not. I'll be hanged if I am. Oh,

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:24.350
don't be so annoying, Waldo. Did you give Mr. Carpenter money? What do you mean? A couple

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:30.000
of checks went through your account endorsed by him. One on May 15th for $250, one on May 22nd for

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:36.980
Oh, that. I asked him to do some shopping for me, that's all.

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:39.020
Shelby's a very obliging fellow.

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:46.490
For some time also, you've been withdrawing various amounts in cash, sometimes 1,500,

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:48.150
sometimes 1,700 at a clip.

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:52.190
Yes, I needed that money.

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:57.340
The day you took out $1,500, Mr. Carpenter deposited 1,350.

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:00.000
When you withdrew 1,700, he deposited 1,500.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:11.330
Maybe they were shooting crap. Oh, must I be insulted like this? I'm sorry, Mrs. Treadwell, but I have to find out about these things.

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:18.200
Shelby needed some money, and I lent it to him. That's all. Well, after all, it is my money. I suppose I can do as I'm pleased with it.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:22.490
Of course. Now, on Friday night, Mrs. Treadwell, you stayed home alone all evening. Yes.

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:30.000
Why didn't you go to the concert with Shelby? Because he didn't ask me. We were just talking about you, Carl.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.210
What a coincidence to find you here.

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:33.210
This is Lieutenant McPherson.

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:35.210
Oh, how do you do, Lieutenant?

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:37.740
I didn't know you were here, Mr. Carpenter.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:39.740
Well, as a matter of fact, I was just lying down here.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:41.740
My hotel room was so hot.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:43.740
And then all the people and reporters and telephone,

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:45.820
you know how it is, Lieutenant.

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:48.010
I've hardly slept a wink since it happened.

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:50.630
Is that a sign of guilt or innocence, McPherson?

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:52.970
I'm at your disposal, Lieutenant.

00:09:52.970 --> 00:09:54.970
I'm as eager to find the murderer as you are.

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:57.970
But what possible motive could I have for killing Laura?

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:00.000
Miss Hunt and I were going to be married this morning,

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.760
week you know no he doesn't know and neither do i or you or anyone else alive what do you mean by

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:11.560
that laura had not definitely made up her mind to marry him she told me so herself last friday when

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:16.000
she called up to cancel our dinner engagement as a matter of fact she was going to the country to

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:21.280
think it over she was extremely kind but i was always sure she would never have thrown her life

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:27.790
away on a male beauty in distress i suppose you've heard losers whine before especially in your

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:30.000
profession eh would you like a bite of lunch

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.300
Now, that's very thoughtful. The perfect host.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:34.800
- Waldo. - You'd almost think he was in his own home.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:38.350
Shelby knows how distracted I am. Would you, Lieutenant?

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:40.630
Thank you, Mrs. Treadwell, but I've got to be going.

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:44.660
But, Lieutenant, I... Well, I rather thought you'd want to ask me some questions.

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:47.930
Oh, yes. What did they play at the concert Friday night?

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:50.530
Oh, Brahms' First and Beethoven's Night.

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:54.150
Uh-uh. Have you got a key to Miss Hunt's house up in the country?

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:56.580
No, but I think there's one up in her apartment.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:59.640
- Okay, well, have a look. - Perhaps I could help you?

00:11:00.730 --> 00:11:02.690
- All right, come along.

00:11:02.690 --> 00:11:28.960
- I'll be seeing you, Mrs. Treadwell.

00:11:57.360 --> 00:11:58.480
The doorbell rang.

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:03.740
And she opened the door, the shop was fired.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:05.760
And how do you deduce that?

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:09.910
Felt backward, I was there.

00:12:09.910 --> 00:12:12.980
I thought you hadn't been up here before.

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:20.400
I saw the police thought of it.

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:26.940
So I better try and find that key.

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:30.000
McPherson, tell me, why did they have

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.850
to photograph her in that horrible condition.

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:34.450
When a dame gets killed, she doesn't worry about how she looks.

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:36.780
Will you stop calling her a dame?

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:38.440
Look around.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:43.150
Is this the home of a dame?

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:48.880
Look at her.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:50.700
Not bad.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:53.720
Jacoby was in love with her when he painted it,

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:57.100
but he never captured her vibrance, her warmth.

00:12:57.100 --> 00:12:59.190
Have you ever been in love?

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:05.640
doll in washington heights once got a fox fur out of me ever know a woman who wasn't a doll or a

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:14.390
dame yeah one but you kept walking me past furniture windows to look at the parlor suites

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:20.340
would you mind turning that off why don't you like it there was one of laura's favorites

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:25.830
not exactly classical but sweet you know a lot about music i don't know a lot about anything

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:28.800
but i know a little about practically everything yeah

00:13:30.030 --> 00:13:31.910
- Why did you say they played Brahms first

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:35.070
and Beethoven's ninth at the concert Friday night?

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:36.490
They changed the program at the last minute

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:37.850
