00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:32.590
Goodbye, Mr. Lidecker.

00:20:33.110 --> 00:20:35.270
If you come a little bit closer, my boy,

00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:37.890
I can just crack your skull with my stick.

00:20:39.510 --> 00:20:45.230
And now, for reasons which are too embarrassing to mention,

00:20:45.950 --> 00:20:47.940
I'd like to endorse that pen.

00:20:48.990 --> 00:20:52.660
Mr. Lidecker, thank you.

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:57.840
You're a very strange man.

00:20:58.080 --> 00:20:58.360
What?

00:20:58.780 --> 00:21:00.000
You're really...

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.880
I'm really sorry for the way you acted, aren't you?

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:10.370
Let's not be psychiatric, Miss Hunt, but in a word, yes.

00:21:10.370 --> 00:21:12.230
It's very kind of you, you know.

00:21:12.230 --> 00:21:16.460
I'm not kind, I'm vicious. It's the secret of my charm.

00:21:16.460 --> 00:21:23.720
But if you choose to think me kind, I'll call for you at six.

00:21:23.720 --> 00:21:29.060
- All right? - All right.

00:21:34.190 --> 00:21:42.040
Her career began with my endorsement, but I secured other endorsements for her, introduced her to important clients.

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:52.050
I gave her her start, but it was her own talent and imagination that enabled her to rise to the top of her profession and stay there.

00:21:52.050 --> 00:22:00.000
She had an eager mind always, but Tuesday and Friday nights we stayed home.

00:22:01.310 --> 00:22:05.710
dining quietly, listening to my records.

00:22:05.710 --> 00:22:08.730
I read my articles to her.

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:14.170
The way she listened was more eloquent than speech.

00:22:14.170 --> 00:22:17.840
These were the best nights.

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:22.420
Then one Tuesday, she phoned and said she couldn't come.

00:22:22.420 --> 00:22:25.000
It didn't matter, really.

00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:27.020
But when it happened again the following Friday,

00:22:27.020 --> 00:22:28.040
I was disturbed.

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:32.440
I couldn't understand it.

00:22:32.440 --> 00:22:38.160
I felt betrayed, and yet I knew Laura would never betray anyone.

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:52.500
Long time, I found myself before her apartment building.

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:54.320
The lights were on.

00:22:54.320 --> 00:22:56.760
It pleased me to know she was home,

00:22:56.760 --> 00:22:59.510
till I saw she was not alone.

00:23:02.270 --> 00:23:14.690
But I waited. I wanted to see who he was. It was Jacoby, who had recently painted her portrait.

00:23:14.690 --> 00:23:21.720
I never liked the man. He was so obviously conscious of looking more like an athlete than an artist.

00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:25.590
I sat up the rest of the night writing a column about him.

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:30.000
I demolished his affectations, exposed his camouflaged imitations of better parents.

00:23:31.050 --> 00:23:38.560
ridicule his theories. I did it for her, knowing Jacoby was unworthy of her. It was a masterpiece,

00:23:38.560 --> 00:23:43.840
because it was a labor of love. Naturally, she could never regard him seriously again.

00:23:45.140 --> 00:23:51.370
There were others, of course, but her own discrimination ruled them out before it became

00:23:51.370 --> 00:24:00.000
necessary for me to intercede, until one night at the party at Anne Treadwell. It was one of her usual

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.500
Roundups of bizarre and nondescript characters.

00:24:02.780 --> 00:24:04.740
Corral from every stratum of society.

00:24:05.120 --> 00:24:06.600
This is Mr. and Mrs. Preston.

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:08.200
They've been waiting all evening to meet you.

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:08.920
How do you do?

00:24:09.920 --> 00:24:13.570
Oh, Shelby.

00:24:13.710 --> 00:24:14.290
Excuse me, honey.

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:20.150
You're Laura Hunt?

00:24:21.710 --> 00:24:22.190
Yes.

00:24:22.710 --> 00:24:22.990
Hello.

00:24:23.810 --> 00:24:24.980
I'm Shelby Carpenter.

00:24:25.260 --> 00:24:25.880
Want to dance?

00:24:26.630 --> 00:24:27.840
I'm not alone.

00:24:30.120 --> 00:24:32.820
Oh, him? I bet he's still doing the poker.

00:24:33.100 --> 00:24:33.820
Excuse me, please.

00:24:34.480 --> 00:24:37.540
Yes, Betsy Ross taught it to me.

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:40.160
Hello, Waldo. Darling, how are you?

00:24:40.280 --> 00:24:41.240
I know you've met Shelby.

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:42.340
Unavoidably.

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:44.700
He was awfully nice to me in Louisville at the Derby.

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:46.180
His family's from Kentucky.

00:24:47.630 --> 00:24:48.590
Sharecroppers, no doubt.

00:24:54.310 --> 00:24:54.750
Louise.

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:57.540
Louise, for the last time, will you marry me?

00:24:57.540 --> 00:25:00.000
I won't, but I saved some chicken livers for you.

00:25:00.060 --> 00:25:03.700
Oh, you're an angel. Say, in the meantime, Darnie, you think you could get this spot out for me?

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:06.740
I can afford a blemish on my character, but not on my clothes.

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:08.240
Mmm, couldn't eat another mouth.

00:25:08.870 --> 00:25:09.740
I'm afraid of cruise.

00:25:10.160 --> 00:25:11.280
I'm afraid of liquor.

00:25:11.770 --> 00:25:13.810
Oh, may I have a glass of milk from Mr. Lidecker?

00:25:13.990 --> 00:25:14.910
Of course, Miss Hunter.

00:25:15.030 --> 00:25:17.090
I've got to tell you, I also read poems.

00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:21.310
I cook, I swallow swords, I mend my own socks and never eat garlic or onions.

00:25:21.430 --> 00:25:22.630
What more can you want of a man?

00:25:23.130 --> 00:25:24.420
Don't listen to that Scallyway.

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:26.380
I didn't expect to find him here, Louise.

00:25:26.380 --> 00:25:28.030
Well, what do you mean? We're old friends.

00:25:28.150 --> 00:25:30.000
She feeds me, humors me, repairs me.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:35.120
me and refuses to marry me, don't you, honey? I do. She has good sense. Wait just a minute.

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:42.060
Thanks, Louise. You're wasting your time. She's got good sense, too. You're jealous. What does

00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:48.690
it feel like, Mr. Carpenter? What does what feel like, Miss Hunt? Living on the income from an

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:55.120
estate. Well, I... Oh, don't you know? Well, I did until the sheriff took it over ten years ago.

00:25:55.740 --> 00:26:00.000
Why maintain the fiction? Why not work? Believe it or not, I asked one of my neighbors,

00:26:00.030 --> 00:26:05.190
friends for a job once, executive of a big company, 2,500 employees. He could have

00:26:05.190 --> 00:26:08.090
pressed the button and done it, but he just laughed. He thought I was joking.

00:26:08.090 --> 00:26:12.610
Weren't you? No. When I convinced him, he got embarrassed. Said he'd phone me. That

00:26:12.610 --> 00:26:17.800
was months ago. Now, whenever he sees me, he looks the other way.

00:26:17.800 --> 00:26:27.520
You really want a job? Yes.

00:26:30.570 --> 00:26:37.930
Here you are Laura dear. I cannot stand these morons any longer if you don't come with me this instant

00:26:37.930 --> 00:26:45.240
I shall run amok. All right, Waldo 10:30 tomorrow bulletin company

00:26:45.700 --> 00:26:51.570
You've got a job. I concealed my annoyance with masterly self-control

00:26:52.250 --> 00:26:56.390
But I sensed a situation which would bear watching

00:26:57.050 --> 00:27:00.000
Laura take a look at this

00:27:01.600 --> 00:27:04.820
Do you like it? Do you think it'll make people want to bathe more often?

00:27:05.410 --> 00:27:07.600
It should. It's excellent.

00:27:07.980 --> 00:27:10.720
There you are. Good night, Miss Hunt. Good night.

00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:12.220
- Good night, Mr. Carleton. - Good night, honey.

00:27:12.220 --> 00:27:14.620
It's really very good. Who's the model?

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:16.220
Oh, a girl named Diane Redfern.

00:27:16.220 --> 00:27:18.220
You hired her yourself last week, don't you remember?

00:27:18.220 --> 00:27:22.220
- Oh, yeah. - Well, let's go, moon of my delight. I am starved.

00:27:22.460 --> 00:27:23.800
You usually are.

00:27:24.920 --> 00:27:26.890
- I approve of that hat. - You do?

00:27:26.890 --> 00:27:28.730
Mm-hmm. And the girl in it, too.

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:35.050
Thank you.

00:27:35.050 --> 00:27:36.870
I knew there was something on my mind.

00:27:36.870 --> 00:27:37.820
What is it?

00:27:37.820 --> 00:27:39.820
Oh yes, will you dine with me tomorrow night?

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:41.030
Maybe.

00:27:41.030 --> 00:27:43.140
No, that isn't what's worrying me.

00:27:43.140 --> 00:27:44.140
It's the next night.

00:27:44.140 --> 00:27:45.140
But Shelby, I don't think...

00:27:45.140 --> 00:27:48.140
Good, and what about three weeks from tonight, and all the nights in between?

00:27:48.140 --> 00:27:50.190
Don't you think I have any other engagement?

00:27:50.190 --> 00:27:53.380
What about two months from now, and the month after that?

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:54.460
What about next year?

00:27:54.460 --> 00:27:55.460
That's all settled.

00:27:55.460 --> 00:27:56.460
What about breakfast?

00:27:56.460 --> 00:27:58.960
What about dancing?

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:30.000
lunch. Beautiful lunches. Day after day after day. What about work? Beautiful work. Day after day after day. Why, Miss Hunt, the way you talk, you'd think I was in love with you. Yes, Laura, I heard everything you said. I went to call for you. I'm sorry, Waldo. And that reminds

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:40.270
me. Old Mother Hubbard has something in the cupboard. The results of my private investigation

00:28:40.270 --> 00:28:47.160
of that sterling character, Mr. Shelby Cobb. By stooping so low, you only degrade yourself,

00:28:47.300 --> 00:28:53.300
Waldo. Did you know that he almost went to jail for passing rubber checks? That he was

00:28:53.300 --> 00:28:57.970
suspected of stealing his hostess jewels when he was a house guest of Virginia? Naturally

00:28:57.970 --> 00:29:00.000
they'd suspect him, because he isn't Brit.

00:29:01.340 --> 00:29:04.910
Those are only insinuations, the cheapest kind.

00:29:04.910 --> 00:29:08.010
These aren't only insinuations, my dear.

00:29:08.010 --> 00:29:09.590
There you are.

00:29:09.590 --> 00:29:11.640
Read them.

00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:14.590
Whatever.

00:29:14.590 --> 00:29:16.110
I know it's false.

00:29:16.110 --> 00:29:18.550
A man can change, can't he?

00:29:18.550 --> 00:29:21.690
People are always ready to hold out a hand to slap you down,

00:29:21.690 --> 00:29:23.710
but never to pick you up.

00:29:23.710 --> 00:29:25.860
All right, I'm helping, Shelby.

00:29:25.860 --> 00:29:27.960
His past is his own affair.

00:29:27.960 --> 00:29:29.670
I only care about the present.

00:29:31.210 --> 00:29:33.850
Speaking of the changed, Mr. Carpenter, in the present tense,

00:29:34.690 --> 00:29:40.850
he's now running around with a model from your own office. Her name is Diane Redburn.

00:29:40.850 --> 00:29:51.400
I'm closer to despising you than I thought I ever would be.

00:29:51.400 --> 00:29:59.600
I should have told you before, Shelby and I are going to be married next week.

00:30:03.930 --> 00:30:06.950
I believe you presented him with a cigarette case on his last birthday.

00:30:10.780 --> 00:30:12.940
Rather valuable, isn't it?

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:14.050
Where did you get it?

00:30:14.730 --> 00:30:17.990
From the pawn shop. Her Diane Redfern took it after he gave it to her.

00:30:18.360 --> 00:30:19.570
I don't believe it.

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:21.680
He probably needed money and was too proud to borrow.

00:30:21.780 --> 00:30:24.160
Carpenter proud?

00:30:25.180 --> 00:30:27.520
Perhaps that's why the pawn ticket was in her name.

00:30:27.820 --> 00:30:29.570
Before this goes any further, I'll just...

00:30:32.010 --> 00:30:43.480
If he isn't home, he's dining at Anne Treadwell's.

00:30:43.480 --> 00:30:45.400
He can't be. He asked me to dinner.

00:30:45.400 --> 00:30:48.960
He would have cancelled his appointment with her if you had accepted.

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:52.360
He treats her rather badly these days.

00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:56.850
I'm afraid she'll say he isn't there.

00:31:05.920 --> 00:31:10.260
Walter, why are you doing this?

00:31:12.860 --> 00:31:16.950
For you, Laura.

00:31:19.880 --> 00:31:21.380
Shall we pay them a visit?

00:31:22.330 --> 00:31:24.190
He won't be there, Walter.

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:26.430
I know he won't.

00:31:26.850 --> 00:31:29.190
All right, we'll see.

00:31:31.760 --> 00:31:34.260
Don't you want to take this little bauble along in case he is there?

00:31:34.260 --> 00:31:46.810
I'm sorry, Mrs. Treadwell is not home.

00:31:46.810 --> 00:31:47.860
We know, dear.

00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:10.740
- Good evening. - I didn't expect to see you tonight.

00:32:10.760 --> 00:32:15.390
There you are, my dear. In a moment of supreme disaster, he's trite.

00:32:15.490 --> 00:32:17.890
You've been reading too many melodramas, Waldo.

00:32:17.920 --> 00:32:21.320
I was just telling Anne about our getting married.

00:32:21.410 --> 00:32:26.460
Well, have you two had dinner? Would you like a glass of wine?

00:32:26.470 --> 00:32:27.910
Why don't you sit down?

00:32:37.480 --> 00:32:40.030
I couldn't find out if she saw carpet in the meantime.

00:32:40.030 --> 00:32:44.880
All I know is that on Friday, she had lunch with Diane Redford.

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:48.690
What came of it, I hoped to hear that night.

00:32:48.690 --> 00:32:52.840
I alternated between moods of over-optimism and over-pessimism.

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:55.640
When the phone rang, I had a foreboding of disaster.

00:33:00.160 --> 00:33:04.280
I called to tell you, Waldo. I'm frightfully sorry. I can't have dinner with you tonight.

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:08.340
Oh, no, no. I'm not sick. I'm just dreadfully nervous.

00:33:08.800 --> 00:33:10.820
I'm going to the country for a few days.

00:33:11.790 --> 00:33:13.510
Yes, I'm afraid it's about Shelby.

00:33:14.860 --> 00:33:16.380
Oh, no, please. There's nothing you can do.

00:33:16.910 --> 00:33:18.720
I've got to think this thing out for myself.

00:33:19.780 --> 00:33:22.630
I'm sorry. I'll call you when I get back.

00:33:23.100 --> 00:33:23.420
Bye.

00:33:24.760 --> 00:33:30.000
For the last time I ever heard her voice, I was sure.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:36.460
- Of course, she had too much pride to forgive him.

00:33:36.460 --> 00:33:39.560
- Where does this Diane Redfern live?

00:33:39.560 --> 00:33:40.780
- Brooklyn somewhere.

00:33:40.780 --> 00:33:44.470
She's in the phone book.

00:33:44.470 --> 00:33:45.310
- That's late.

00:33:45.310 --> 00:33:47.240
Let's get going.

00:33:47.240 --> 00:33:48.510
- Waiter.

00:33:48.510 --> 00:33:52.520
- Yes, sir.

00:33:52.520 --> 00:33:53.360
- All right.

00:33:53.360 --> 00:33:54.130
- Thank you, Mr. Lidecker.

00:34:04.640 --> 00:34:08.930
I shall never forgive myself for letting her become involved with Shelby.

00:34:08.930 --> 00:34:13.770
It was my fault. I should have stopped it somehow.

00:34:13.770 --> 00:34:21.000
Well, it's too late now. Thanks for the wine.

00:34:40.400 --> 00:34:45.410
Hello, Masconeys. This is Lieutenant McPherson, Homicide Bureau.

00:34:45.410 --> 00:34:49.730
Laura Hunt had been buying her liquor from you for several years, hadn't she?

00:34:49.730 --> 00:34:51.870
Yeah.

00:34:51.870 --> 00:34:55.600
Did she ever buy a brand of scotch called Black Pony?

00:34:55.600 --> 00:34:57.940
Yeah.

00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:05.080
I see. Well, that's all I wanted to know, thanks.

00:35:05.080 --> 00:35:06.580
She's here, McCleson, the maid.

00:35:06.580 --> 00:35:10.820
Okay. Come in, Miss Clary.

00:35:10.820 --> 00:35:15.770
Never mind the Miss Clary stuff. My name's Bessie, and I'm a domestic, and I got nothing to be ashamed of.

00:35:15.770 --> 00:35:17.360
Sit down, Bessie.

00:35:17.360 --> 00:35:27.130
Her letters and her private diary. You've been reading them, pawing over them.

00:35:27.130 --> 00:35:30.000
It's a shame in the face of the dead. That's what it is. It's a shame.

00:35:30.430 --> 00:35:31.280
Sit down, Bessie.

00:35:31.400 --> 00:35:32.980
I'll stand on my own two feet.

00:35:33.420 --> 00:35:34.780
Don't you go ordering me around.

00:35:35.320 --> 00:35:36.460
I ain't afraid of cops.

00:35:36.930 --> 00:35:39.080
I was brought up to spit whenever I saw one.

00:35:40.020 --> 00:35:40.530
Okay.

00:35:41.300 --> 00:35:42.630
Go ahead and spit.

00:35:42.750 --> 00:35:43.610
That'll make you feel better.

00:35:44.780 --> 00:35:50.820
What do you want to know?

00:35:51.360 --> 00:35:53.040
What we all want to know.

00:35:53.180 --> 00:35:54.080
Who killed Laura Hunt.

00:35:54.430 --> 00:35:55.360
How would I know?

00:35:55.360 --> 00:35:59.190
He don't think I done it.

00:36:00.480 --> 00:36:03.290
- I know you cops get crazy notions,

00:36:03.290 --> 00:36:05.670
but if you got any notion concerning me,

00:36:05.670 --> 00:36:09.420
ask anyone, anyone who ever come to this house,

00:36:09.420 --> 00:36:11.220
I would have worked for her.

00:36:11.220 --> 00:36:13.140
I would have washed, iron, scrub,

00:36:13.140 --> 00:36:14.840
done everything she wanted of me,

00:36:14.840 --> 00:36:17.040
whether she paid me for it or not.

00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:18.360
And it wasn't only on account

00:36:18.360 --> 00:36:21.170
of the thousand sweet things she'd done for me.

00:36:21.170 --> 00:36:23.680
It was because she was so sweet herself,

00:36:23.680 --> 00:36:28.260
because she was a real fine lady.

00:36:28.260 --> 00:36:30.000
But you cops wouldn't know about that.

00:36:32.270 --> 00:36:38.200
you do. That's all the more reason why you should help me, Bessie. Do you happen to know

00:36:38.200 --> 00:36:45.400
how this got into her liquor cabinet? I put it there. But she never bought cheap stuff

00:36:45.400 --> 00:36:51.900
like that. Not a lady like Miss Hunt. No. When did you put it in the cabinet? Saturday.

00:36:51.900 --> 00:37:00.000
Before the police came. Was it there Friday night before you left? No. Are you sure of

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:05.990
Friday and put the empties in the basement. Then somebody was with her in

00:37:05.990 --> 00:37:11.690
the apartment Friday night, someone who brought that bottle? Yes. Who? I don't know.

00:37:11.690 --> 00:37:16.600
But I didn't want anyone getting any wrong ideas about her. God rest her soul.

00:37:16.600 --> 00:37:20.670
That's why I took it out of the bedroom and put it in the cabinet before the

00:37:20.670 --> 00:37:26.130
police got here. And that ain't all I done. I washed out the glasses and

00:37:26.130 --> 00:37:30.000
cleaned off the bottle too. Do you know what happens to people who destroy evidence?

00:37:30.090 --> 00:37:31.280
I don't care.

00:37:33.040 --> 00:37:35.750
You ain't gonna tell the newspaper reporters, are you?

00:37:35.850 --> 00:37:39.750
And let them make up their nasty stories and drag her name through the mud?

00:37:41.010 --> 00:37:43.380
Go ahead, but it won't do you any good.

00:37:43.420 --> 00:37:45.340
I'll say you lied. I'll say you come here--

00:37:45.420 --> 00:37:46.780
Take it easy, Bessie.

00:37:47.350 --> 00:37:49.140
Get me some ice and a set up, will you?

00:37:49.670 --> 00:37:50.760
All right.

00:37:52.040 --> 00:37:53.720
A couple of highball glasses.

00:37:57.010 --> 00:38:00.000
Good morning.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:01.000
Good morning, Lieutenant.

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:02.000
Hello, McPherson.

00:38:02.000 --> 00:38:04.000
This is quite a delegation.

00:38:04.000 --> 00:38:05.000
I only sent for you, Carpenter.

00:38:05.000 --> 00:38:06.000
I know.

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:07.000
Shelby's dropping me off at the hairdressers later,

00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:09.000
so I thought I might as well come along.

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:11.000
My excuse is equally feeble.

00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:13.020
I just popped in to pay my dubious respects

00:38:13.020 --> 00:38:15.020
and inquires to the state of your health.

00:38:15.020 --> 00:38:16.550
Insipid, I trust.

00:38:16.550 --> 00:38:17.550
I was just going to pour myself a drink.

00:38:17.550 --> 00:38:18.550
Would you care to join me?

00:38:18.550 --> 00:38:20.550
Well, that's a very nice idea, Shelby.

00:38:20.550 --> 00:38:21.550
Wouldn't you like one?

00:38:21.550 --> 00:38:22.550
I'll get it.

00:38:22.550 --> 00:38:23.530
Never mind.

00:38:23.530 --> 00:38:24.530
Bessie, would you bring a couple more glasses, please?

00:38:24.530 --> 00:38:25.530
Yes, sir.

00:38:25.530 --> 00:38:26.530
Hi, Bessie.

00:38:26.530 --> 00:38:27.480
What are you doing here?

00:38:27.480 --> 00:38:29.380
I'm paid up for the week and I'm working.

00:38:29.380 --> 00:38:30.000
and I'm working.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:34.580
regardless. Would you like one, Lidegger? I see no reason to exclude myself. The host

00:38:34.580 --> 00:38:39.590
provides Scotch. Does it do? I presume it'll have to. How about you, Carpenter? It's cheap,

00:38:39.590 --> 00:38:43.430
but it's potent. As a matter of fact, I don't think I care for any. I'm not much of a daytime

00:38:43.430 --> 00:38:51.080
drinker. That'll be all for you, Bessie. You can go home now. But I... Yes, sir. I remember when

00:38:51.080 --> 00:38:55.200
Laura bought these glasses. She loved them. She loved all her things, sir. What are you

00:38:55.200 --> 00:38:59.700
going to do? Sell them? I don't know. I suppose, sir. Thank you.

00:39:00.190 --> 00:39:04.260
If I'm appointed administrator of the estate, I shall probably just call in Corey.

00:39:04.260 --> 00:39:06.470
You mean, uh, Lancaster Corey, the art dealer?

00:39:06.470 --> 00:39:08.670
Yes, he was a friend of Laura's.

00:39:08.670 --> 00:39:11.960
Let him dispose of everything. It'll be less gruesome that way.

00:39:11.960 --> 00:39:15.020
Not quite everything, Anne.

00:39:15.020 --> 00:39:17.480
Two or three things in here belong to me.

00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:20.090
This vase, for instance.

00:39:20.090 --> 00:39:25.770
And that, uh, the clock, of course, and the antique fire screen.

00:39:25.770 --> 00:39:27.010
I will lend them to Laura, you know.

00:39:27.010 --> 00:39:28.310
Oh, really, Waldo?

00:39:28.310 --> 00:39:29.120
Yes, really.

00:39:30.030 --> 00:39:32.470
The vase is the gem of my collection. I intend to have it back.

00:39:32.470 --> 00:39:33.850
And the clock on the screen, too.

00:39:33.850 --> 00:39:36.340
But they aren't yours. You gave them to Lara. I won't permit it.

00:39:36.340 --> 00:39:39.540
Doesn't alleged fiancé have any voice in this matter?

00:39:39.540 --> 00:39:44.790
I'll take the vase with me now and send someone to collect the other things this very day.

00:39:44.790 --> 00:39:48.560
Nothing is leaving here except you, Lideker.

00:39:48.560 --> 00:39:51.830
Is that your quaint way of indicating dismissal?

00:39:51.830 --> 00:39:55.810
We're all going anyway. I have to be back at headquarters by noon.

00:39:55.810 --> 00:39:58.470
But, Lieutenant, I don't understand. You sent for me, didn't you?

00:40:00.250 --> 00:40:03.590
- Yeah? - Well, don't you want to see me? Don't you want to ask me some questions?

00:40:03.670 --> 00:40:05.130
- I'll be seeing you. - Well...

00:40:05.240 --> 00:40:07.870
- I bid you good day. - Come along, Shelby.

00:40:07.950 --> 00:40:13.350
- Are you making any progress on the case, Lieutenant? - We're doing all right.

00:40:31.340 --> 00:40:33.340
We'll get something to eat, I'll take over.

00:40:33.340 --> 00:40:34.390
Thanks.

00:40:34.390 --> 00:40:36.980
Take your time.

00:42:03.160 --> 00:42:03.620
Thank you.

00:43:44.110 --> 00:43:46.830
Does Mac Fisson, any calls come through?

00:43:46.830 --> 00:43:49.370
Well, keep listening.

00:43:49.370 --> 00:43:51.570
No, nothing new.

00:43:51.570 --> 00:44:00.000
I happen to see the lights on.

00:44:00.920 --> 00:44:06.880
- Have you sublet this apartment? You're here often enough to pay rent. - Any objections?

00:44:06.880 --> 00:44:14.870
- Yes. I object to you prying into Laura's letters. Especially those from me. - Why? Yours are the best in the bunch.

00:44:14.870 --> 00:44:24.910
- Thanks. But I didn't write them to you. Haven't you any sense of privacy? - Murder victims have no claim to privacy.

00:44:24.910 --> 00:44:28.870
- Have detectives who buy portraits of murder victims a claim to privacy?

00:44:31.210 --> 00:44:34.200
Lancaster Corey told me that you've already put in a bid for it.

00:44:34.200 --> 00:44:37.110
That's none of your business.

00:44:37.110 --> 00:44:41.130
Macpherson, did it ever strike you that you're acting very strangely?

00:44:41.130 --> 00:44:44.820
It's a wonder you don't come here like a suitor with roses and a box of candy.

00:44:44.820 --> 00:44:47.590
Duck store candy, of course.

00:44:47.590 --> 00:44:50.510
Have you ever dreamed of Laura as your wife?

00:44:50.510 --> 00:44:53.830
By your side at the policeman's ball or in the bleachers?

00:44:53.830 --> 00:44:58.450
Or listening to the heroic story of how you got a silver shin bone from a gun battle with a gangster?

00:45:00.890 --> 00:45:04.540
-I see you have. -Why don't you go home? I'm busy.

00:45:04.540 --> 00:45:08.970
Perhaps we can come to terms now.

00:45:08.970 --> 00:45:10.360
You want the portrait?

00:45:10.360 --> 00:45:12.340
Perfectly understandable.

00:45:12.340 --> 00:45:15.800
I want my possessions, my bars, my clock and my screen.

00:45:15.800 --> 00:45:17.770
Also perfectly understandable.

00:45:17.770 --> 00:45:20.220
-Now if you... -Get going.

00:45:20.220 --> 00:45:24.830
You better watch out, Macpherson, or you'll end up in a psychiatric ward.

00:45:24.830 --> 00:45:28.420
I don't think they've ever had a patient who fell in love with a corpse.

00:46:59.400 --> 00:47:00.000
What are you doing?

00:47:06.620 --> 00:47:08.520
If you don't get out at once, I'm going to call the police.

00:47:09.040 --> 00:47:10.840
You are Laura Hunt, aren't you?

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:14.800
Aren't you?

00:47:15.350 --> 00:47:16.650
I'm going to call the police.

00:47:17.040 --> 00:47:18.100
But I am the police.

00:47:19.560 --> 00:47:20.300
Mark McPherson.

00:47:21.530 --> 00:47:24.010
What's this all about?

00:47:26.130 --> 00:47:26.630
Don't you know?

00:47:27.550 --> 00:47:28.290
Don't you know what's happened?

00:47:28.770 --> 00:47:29.380
No.

00:47:31.180 --> 00:47:33.880
Haven't you seen the papers? Where have you been?

00:47:34.420 --> 00:47:37.910
Up in the country. I don't get a newspaper.

00:47:38.350 --> 00:47:42.220
- Haven't you got a radio? - It was broken.

00:48:02.460 --> 00:48:03.940
Somebody was murdered in this room.

00:48:05.260 --> 00:48:06.600
Do you have any idea who it was?

00:48:07.240 --> 00:48:07.730
No.

00:48:09.190 --> 00:48:10.390
Who had a key to your apartment?

00:48:11.700 --> 00:48:12.360
Nobody.

00:48:14.180 --> 00:48:14.540
You're sure?

00:48:15.240 --> 00:48:17.700
When did it happen?

00:48:18.690 --> 00:48:19.250
Friday night.

00:48:20.710 --> 00:48:24.650
What are you going to do now?

00:48:25.570 --> 00:48:28.950
Find out who was murdered, and then find the murderer.

00:48:33.800 --> 00:48:35.120
You better take off those wet clothes.

00:48:35.590 --> 00:48:36.470
They might catch cold.

00:48:41.640 --> 00:48:54.140
Yeah, I found this in my closet.

00:48:55.040 --> 00:48:55.960
It's Diane Redford.

00:48:56.460 --> 00:48:57.990
It wasn't here when I left.

00:48:58.630 --> 00:48:59.580
She's one of our models.

00:49:00.410 --> 00:49:19.310
just about my size beautiful wasn't she do you suppose sit down please this is monday night

00:49:19.310 --> 00:49:29.820
you left on friday rather a long weekend isn't it yes what train did you take 726 see anybody you

00:49:30.000 --> 00:50:00.000
the train? No. Then what? Then I got off the train at Norwalk. So nobody you knew at the station either? No. Go on. Then I went to the garage where I keep my car. It's a private garage. Nobody saw me there either. Then I drove to my house. You were there three days. What did you do? Worked in my garden. You didn't go out in all that time? No. I had every

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:01.200
I needed in the house.

00:50:01.200 --> 00:50:03.230
Nobody came to see you?

00:50:03.230 --> 00:50:04.500
Nobody.

00:50:04.500 --> 00:50:06.180
I went there to be alone.

00:50:06.180 --> 00:50:08.230
Police were there on Saturday.

00:50:08.230 --> 00:50:09.530
There was no one in the house.

00:50:09.530 --> 00:50:11.630
Oh, yes.

00:50:11.630 --> 00:50:13.650
Saturday, I took a long walk.

00:50:13.650 --> 00:50:16.140
I walked for hours in the woods.

00:50:16.140 --> 00:50:17.730
Mm-hmm.

00:50:17.730 --> 00:50:21.320
You were going to marry Shelby Carpenter this week.

00:50:21.320 --> 00:50:23.520
Thursday, if I'm not mistaken.

00:50:23.520 --> 00:50:27.030
Yes.

00:50:27.030 --> 00:50:29.990
Yet you went away just before your wedding.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:37.390
for a long weekend to be alone. I was tired. I'd been working hard. You know Shelby Carpenter

00:50:37.390 --> 00:50:42.190
has a key to this apartment. Why didn't you tell me? I know nothing of the sort. He hasn't.

00:50:42.190 --> 00:50:45.980
How else did the girl get into the apartment? You knew she was in love with Carpenter, that

00:50:45.980 --> 00:50:49.920
he had given her your cigarette case. You know all that, don't you? I knew that she

00:50:49.920 --> 00:50:55.610
was in love with him. She told me so herself. When did she tell you? At lunch last Friday.

00:50:55.610 --> 00:51:00.000
I also know that she meant nothing to Shelby. I understand him better than you do. She was

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:01.220
and your dressing gown and slippers.

00:51:01.740 --> 00:51:03.160
That's hardly the regulation costume

00:51:03.160 --> 00:51:05.100
for an impersonal chat between a man and woman

00:51:05.100 --> 00:51:06.280
who mean nothing to each other.

00:51:07.100 --> 00:51:08.200
Did you know or did you suspect

00:51:08.200 --> 00:51:10.100
that he was going to bring her here Friday night, Miss Hunt?

00:51:10.180 --> 00:51:10.780
How could I?

00:51:10.820 --> 00:51:11.940
I don't know that he brought her here.

00:51:12.000 --> 00:51:12.600
Neither do you.

00:51:12.940 --> 00:51:13.800
You merely assume it.

00:51:13.840 --> 00:51:15.080
What other assumption is possible?

00:51:15.470 --> 00:51:16.850
Do you love this fellow carpenter so much

00:51:16.850 --> 00:51:18.350
you risk your own safety to protect him?

00:51:18.780 --> 00:51:19.580
My own safety?

00:51:20.100 --> 00:51:20.940
You suspect me?

00:51:21.520 --> 00:51:23.160
I suspect nobody and everybody.

00:51:23.520 --> 00:51:24.620
I'm merely trying to get at the truth.

00:51:25.420 --> 00:51:27.120
I see you have been trying to get at the truth.

00:51:27.700 --> 00:51:30.000
You've read things I never met anyone else to look at.

00:51:30.890 --> 00:51:35.000
Strictly routine. I'm sorry, really.

00:51:35.000 --> 00:51:40.800
I'd better be going. I'll see you in the morning, Miss Hunt.

00:51:40.800 --> 00:51:43.920
Oh, I must ask you not to leave the house or use the phone.

00:51:43.920 --> 00:51:47.770
But I've got to let my friends know I'm alive.

00:51:47.770 --> 00:51:50.450
Sorry, Miss Hunt, but I must insist you do as I say.

00:51:50.450 --> 00:51:51.930
Am I under arrest?

00:51:51.930 --> 00:51:58.380
No. If anything should happen to you this time, I wouldn't like it.

00:51:58.380 --> 00:52:00.000
All right, I promise.

00:52:00.320 --> 00:52:03.190
There's one more thing. You may as well know what I know.

00:52:03.530 --> 00:52:04.430
Some of it, at any rate.

00:52:05.070 --> 00:52:07.370
It'll save time and a lot of unnecessary fencing.

00:52:08.820 --> 00:52:11.140
I know that you went away to make up your mind

00:52:11.140 --> 00:52:17.730
whether you would marry Shelby Carpenter or not.

00:52:18.770 --> 00:52:21.700
What did you decide? I want the truth.

00:52:23.710 --> 00:52:25.440
I decided not to marry him.

00:52:27.610 --> 00:52:29.200
I'll see you in the morning. Good night.

00:52:30.190 --> 00:52:44.730
Good night.

00:52:44.730 --> 00:52:47.420
Really?

00:52:47.420 --> 00:52:50.610
Does McPherson know that?

00:52:50.610 --> 00:52:53.120
Bye.

00:52:53.120 --> 00:52:56.420
Say, Mark, that was the medical examiner's report.

00:52:56.420 --> 00:52:57.580
It wasn't Laura Hunt.

00:52:57.580 --> 00:52:59.860
It was Diane Redfern that was bumped off upstairs.

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:03.980
of balls things up, doesn't it? Yeah. Say, you're not taking it too hard.

00:53:08.440 --> 00:53:13.680
Me right away. I'll wait for you in my car in front of bullets.

00:53:15.120 --> 00:53:18.540
What is that? Yeah. Dames are always pulling a switch

00:53:18.540 --> 00:53:20.300
on you. Stand by.

00:53:39.080 --> 00:53:40.720
Follow her, I'm gonna tail him.

00:54:33.550 --> 00:54:49.470
- Are you taking it down or putting it away, Carpenter?

00:54:49.470 --> 00:54:50.410
- Fired lately?

00:54:50.410 --> 00:54:52.130
- Yes, I killed some rabbits with it.

00:54:52.130 --> 00:54:52.970
- When?

00:54:52.970 --> 00:54:54.900
- Oh, a while back, I don't know exactly.

00:54:54.900 --> 00:54:56.810
- You know about guns, don't you?

00:54:56.810 --> 00:54:58.250
- Yes.

00:54:58.250 --> 00:54:59.800
- How come you didn't clean it afterward?

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:02.140
I don't know, I forgot, I suppose.

00:55:02.140 --> 00:55:03.400
Your initials?

00:55:03.400 --> 00:55:05.900
Yes, I gave it to Laura for protection.

00:55:05.900 --> 00:55:07.730
She didn't want it, but I insisted.

00:55:07.730 --> 00:55:11.370
This house is rather isolated, as you see.

00:55:11.370 --> 00:55:12.370
Did you teach her how to use it?

00:55:12.370 --> 00:55:13.200
No.

00:55:13.200 --> 00:55:14.690
Does she know how?

00:55:14.690 --> 00:55:16.690
I don't know, it didn't occur to me to ask.

00:55:16.690 --> 00:55:20.250
You're a vague sort of a fellow, aren't you, Carpenter?

00:55:20.250 --> 00:55:23.250
I've spent very little time in observing my own character, Mr. McPherson.

00:55:23.250 --> 00:55:26.250
You haven't borrowed it lately, you didn't just bring it back tonight.

00:55:26.250 --> 00:55:29.570
You followed me here, you saw me come in, you ought to know.

00:55:30.350 --> 00:55:34.210
You realize the spot you're in, Carpenter?

00:55:34.210 --> 00:55:36.390
You took that poor girl to Miss Hunt's apartment.

00:55:36.390 --> 00:55:40.700
You knew all along it was she who was murdered.

00:55:40.700 --> 00:55:42.620
Didn't you know Laura Hunt would come back any day

00:55:42.620 --> 00:55:45.990
and spill the whole thing?

00:55:45.990 --> 00:55:47.670
Or did you plan to kill her too,

00:55:47.670 --> 00:55:50.850
hide the body someplace, and cover up your first crime?

00:55:50.850 --> 00:55:54.490
You're being fantastic, McPherson.

00:55:54.490 --> 00:55:57.370
You took a bottle of Black Pony to her house Friday night.

00:55:57.370 --> 00:56:00.000
I took it there over a week ago, Bessie said.

00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:02.540
It says it wasn't there Friday night. It was Saturday morning.

00:56:02.540 --> 00:56:04.910
I can't help what Bessie said.

00:56:04.910 --> 00:56:09.320
Where is the key to her apartment?

00:56:09.320 --> 00:56:10.310
I haven't got it.

00:56:10.310 --> 00:56:12.740
What did you do with it? Give it back to her tonight?

00:56:12.740 --> 00:56:15.320
I never had one.

00:56:15.320 --> 00:56:18.080
Okay, you didn't take the bottle of scotch to her house Friday night,

00:56:18.080 --> 00:56:20.460
and you haven't got a key to her apartment. How did you get in?

00:56:20.460 --> 00:56:20.880
Well, I...

00:56:20.880 --> 00:56:23.660
You had a key, and I know it. Now, come on, Carpenter, spill it.

00:56:23.660 --> 00:56:25.040
I...

00:56:25.040 --> 00:56:29.060
Laura kept a duplicate key at her office. I went over and got it.

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:34.520
asked Diane to dine with me. I wanted to have it out with her once and for all. You know, she thought,

00:56:34.520 --> 00:56:40.720
well, she thought she was in love with me. Well, we couldn't go on talking in public places. She was

00:56:40.720 --> 00:56:45.840
too upset. I couldn't go to her room, and I didn't want to take her to my hotel, so we went to Laura's.

00:56:45.960 --> 00:56:53.600
Yeah? We talked there for about three hours, and then the doorbell rang, and... Go on. Diane was frightened,

00:56:53.800 --> 00:56:56.840
but knowing Laura's, I've known her, I've learned to be surprised at nothing.

00:56:57.530 --> 00:57:00.000
What do you mean by that? Well, her friends would come

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:02.240
to her with her troubles at all hours of the day or night.

00:57:03.120 --> 00:57:05.230
I asked Diane to answer the door.

00:57:05.690 --> 00:57:06.690
Why didn't you go yourself?

00:57:06.870 --> 00:57:09.240
Well, supposing one of Laura's friends had found me there.

00:57:09.560 --> 00:57:10.660
Why open the door at all?

00:57:10.740 --> 00:57:11.860
They must have seen the light.

00:57:12.390 --> 00:57:14.090
What about the girl, Diane?

00:57:14.170 --> 00:57:15.250
What about her reputation?

00:57:15.330 --> 00:57:16.970
Well, I asked her to tell them that Laura had

00:57:16.970 --> 00:57:18.790
entered the apartment while she was away.

00:57:19.180 --> 00:57:21.080
Anybody that knew Laura would have believed that.

00:57:21.490 --> 00:57:22.110
Yeah, go on.

00:57:22.580 --> 00:57:24.260
Well, then the doorbell rang again.

00:57:24.860 --> 00:57:26.400
I could hear Diane's mules,

00:57:27.070 --> 00:57:30.000
Laura's they were, clattering across the bare boards between the rugs.

00:57:30.890 --> 00:57:38.450
then there was a moment's silence and then a shot. It was an awful explosion. By the time I reached

00:57:38.450 --> 00:57:43.900
her, the door was closed. She lay there on the floor. You got to see who it was? No, I was too

00:57:43.900 --> 00:57:48.940
confused, too horrified, incapable of doing anything. The room was dark. I saw only a vague

00:57:48.940 --> 00:57:55.540
heap lying on the floor. I don't think I fully grasped the situation. I think I called her name,

00:57:55.640 --> 00:58:00.000
but I'm not sure. I remember kneeling on the floor, feeling her.

00:58:00.030 --> 00:58:07.500
heart. My first instinct was to call the police. Why didn't you? I don't know. Rather, I was afraid,

00:58:07.500 --> 00:58:12.530
not only for myself, but for Laura. In a panicky sort of way, I felt that I must keep out of this,

00:58:12.590 --> 00:58:17.880
to keep Laura out of it. Oh, I know now how foolish and hopeless it was, but there was only one thing

00:58:17.880 --> 00:58:23.220
on my mind. The safety of a person whose life was dearer to me than my own. Don't you understand that?

00:58:23.880 --> 00:58:28.940
Did you think Laura had done it? Did you? I don't remember what I thought.

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:31.590
You think so now?

00:58:31.590 --> 00:58:32.670
No.

00:58:32.670 --> 00:58:33.670
But you didn't.

00:58:33.670 --> 00:58:35.510
No.

00:58:35.510 --> 00:58:38.510
On Saturday, when our men went to the hotel to tell you that Laura was dead, you seemed

00:58:38.510 --> 00:58:39.510
sincerely shocked.

00:58:39.510 --> 00:58:40.510
I was.

00:58:40.510 --> 00:58:41.510
I hadn't expected that mistake.

00:58:41.510 --> 00:58:44.510
But you had your alibi ready, no matter who was dead.

00:58:44.510 --> 00:58:46.510
Bet you knew the minute Laura got back, it wouldn't stick.

00:58:46.510 --> 00:58:47.720
Don't you see?

00:58:47.720 --> 00:58:50.080
I was incapable of thinking that far ahead.

00:58:50.080 --> 00:58:52.720
I was incapable of thinking at all.

00:58:52.720 --> 00:58:55.700
I was groping for some way to keep Laura's name out of it.

00:58:55.700 --> 00:59:00.000
I was heartbroken about Diane and panic-stricken about Laura.

00:59:00.830 --> 00:59:04.630
- I haven't slept a full two hours since this thing happened. - Let's get back to the present.

00:59:04.920 --> 00:59:09.290
- What did you and Laura talk about tonight? - I told her the whole story, just as I told you.

00:59:11.050 --> 00:59:14.190
- She phoned you after she promised me she wouldn't call anybody. What did she want?

00:59:14.430 --> 00:59:18.150
- It's perfectly natural she should want to see me, especially after what's happened.

00:59:18.530 --> 00:59:24.520
- Why don't you tell the truth, carpenter? She sent her here to get rid of this gun. - She did not. It was my own idea. She doesn't even know I'm here.

00:59:31.310 --> 00:59:32.960
- It works fine, doesn't it?

00:59:32.960 --> 00:59:35.800
- Yes, did you think it wouldn't?

00:59:35.800 --> 00:59:37.400
